# cp .env.example .env
#
# READ THE DISK LINE BEFORE YOU START. A full mainnet bmc datadir with the default indexes is
# measured at about 1.2 TB and the first sync takes roughly a day on fast NVMe and a fast CPU
# (18 h 29 m, measured 2026-09-22 on a 16-core machine with an NVMe drive; a slower disk or
# fewer cores is a multi-day job). This is not a package you try out on a laptop SSD.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- what gets built
# Both pins are commits measured to work together. Moving either is a deliberate act.
BMC_REF=fbb22e2303544eba955b6eb33c65b8fa6a8122b4
BLOCKYARD_REF=6a8263bcf7517632ae811344d16739daf33238bd
IMAGE=blockyard-bmc:dev

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the chain
# main | testnet4 | signet | regtest   (bmc has no testnet3)
#
# MAINNET BY DEFAULT, deliberately: the point of this package is that bmc's claim -- a UTXO set
# identical to Core's, from genesis -- is checkable by other people, and nobody checks it on a
# chain they do not care about. See the A/B section below to put your own Core node on the same
# page. testnet4 and signet are the small chains if you want to try the package itself first;
# they fit on an ordinary disk, where mainnet does not.
BMC_CHAIN=main

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- ports
BMC_P2P_PORT=8333
BLOCKYARD_PORT=21000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the monitor
# The first admin account's password. Leave empty and BlockYard generates one and prints it
# once, in the monitor container's log.
BLOCKYARD_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
BLOCKYARD_AUTH=1
BLOCKYARD_TLS=0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the node
BMC_DBCACHE=1024
BMC_MAXCONNECTIONS=48

# The indexes, which ARE the disk footprint. Rough mainnet figures measured on the production
# node, 2026-09-24:
#   the chain itself (blk/undo)   unavoidable          ~950 GB
#   addrindex + address history   addr_hist + tail     ~240 GB   leave ON: see below
#   txindex                       txindex.*             ~26 GB
#   coinstatsindex                coinstats.*            ~2 GB
#   blockfilterindex              bfilters.*            ~13 GB   OFF by default: nothing here reads it
#
# addrindex ON is what lets the MONITOR skip building its own address index -- that would be a
# second ~124 GB copy and half an hour of build. Turning it off to save space costs more space.
BMC_ADDRINDEX=1
BMC_TXINDEX=1
BMC_COINSTATSINDEX=1
BMC_BLOCKFILTERINDEX=0

# Anything else, verbatim, one setting per line (see bmc's config/bitcoin.sample.conf):
#   BMC_EXTRA_CONF="assumevalid=0\nprune=0"
BMC_EXTRA_CONF=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- A/B against your own Core
# Point the monitor at a Bitcoin Core you ALREADY RUN and it appears beside bmc: same page,
# same charts, same picker. Nothing is started for you. Leave CORE_RPC_URL empty to watch bmc
# alone.
#
# Reaching Core from inside the container: a LAN address works as-is; a Core on this host is
# http://host.docker.internal:8332 (add `extra_hosts: ["host.docker.internal:host-gateway"]`
# to the blockyard service on Linux), or just use the host's LAN address.
#CORE_RPC_URL=http://192.0.2.10:8332
#CORE_RPC_USER=
#CORE_RPC_PASSWORD=
#CORE_LABEL=Bitcoin Core
#
# Cookie auth instead of a user/password needs Core's datadir readable in the container, which
# is what docker-compose.core.yml mounts:
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.core.yml up -d
#CORE_DATADIR_HOST=/var/lib/bitcoind
#CORE_COOKIE_FILE=/core/.cookie
#
# NOT set for you: an addressIndex for the Core node. Core has no address index of its own, so
# naming one would start BlockYard building a ~124 GB copy from Core's block files. Add it to
# the rendered config by hand if you want it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- watching a sync
# The monitor polls the node throughout its initial sync -- that is the point of the package.
#
# BMC_BOOT_CATCHUP=0 is what makes that possible, and it is NOT bmc's default. Measured on
# mainnet from an empty datadir, 2026-09-24: with bmc's default (1) the parallel block download
# runs inside the boot phase and the RPC server starts only after it, so nothing answers RPC
# until the whole sync is done -- eighteen hours and more of a node that is working and looks
# unreachable. With it 0, boot completes in 0.07 s and the chain RPCs are live immediately.
#
# TIMING A SYNC RATHER THAN WATCHING ONE? Just stop the monitor (docker compose stop
# blockyard) -- leave this at 0. Corrected 2026-09-24: 0 is what every published bmc benchmark
# already used (validation/fresh_ibd_run.sh writes it; runs 27, 28 and 29 all carry it in their
# captured configs, all three MuHash-identical to Core), and bmc made it the default the same
# day. The polling is the only thing that makes a watched sync a poor stopwatch.
BMC_BOOT_CATCHUP=0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the network
# Fixed so the node's rpcallowip can name it exactly. Change it if it collides with something
# on the host; the two settings move together.
SUBNET=172.31.7.0/24
